sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/aristotle-ha08-02> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Nature proceeds little by little from lifeless things through plants to animals, so that no exact boundary can be drawn and intermediate forms cannot be assigned to one side or the other.";
    dc:type "conceptual";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Nature passes by degrees from lifeless things to animals with no sharp boundary";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Aristotle, The History of Animals (Historia Animalium), Book VIII, Part 1; translated by D'Arcy Wentworth Thompson, The Works of Aristotle Translated into English, ed. J. A. Smith and W. D. Ross, Oxford: Clarendon Press, vol. IV (1910). Scope: all living and non-living things; intermediate forms such as sponges, ascidians, sea-anemones and sessile shellfish"
}
